<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="teams-and-access"
      updated="July 31, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"roles", "Roles"},
        {"inviting", "Inviting your team"},
        {"runner-scopes", "Per-member scope: runners and packs"},
        {"mfa", "MFA"},
        {"sessions", "Sessions & credentials"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="teams-and-access">
        <:dek>
          Who can see what, who can run what, who can approve what — and how to narrow any of it
          down to specific runners. emisar's access model has two dials: the member's
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">role</strong>
          and optional <strong class="text-zinc-200">per-member runner scopes</strong>. An API key
          an LLM connects with carries no scope of its own — it inherits its owner's, so scoping
          the person scopes their agent.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/team-page.webp"
        title="emisar console · Team"
        caption="The roster — each member's role, runner scope, and a directory-sync badge — beside the account's security panel."
        alt="The desktop Team page: the member roster — each member with their role (owner, admin, operator, viewer), their runner scope (all runners, or a specific group), and a badge on members provisioned and role-synced through SCIM — beside the Security panel with 2FA enforcement and single sign-on."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="roles">Roles</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Four ordered roles plus one specialist seat. Authorization is permission-based under
        the hood, so this table is the contract:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Can do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Owner</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Everything an admin can, plus billing and the account itself. Only owners manage
                other owners, and the last owner can't be removed or demoted — accounts can't be
                orphaned.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Manage the team (invite, change roles, suspend), policies, runbooks, runner
                fleet, pack trust, API keys, and runner scopes. Reads the invoices without being
                able to change the plan. Plus everything below.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Billing manager</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The finance seat: manage the subscription, payment method, and invoices —
                and nothing else. No team, runner, policy, or action access, so procurement
                can own billing without touching infrastructure.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Operator</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Day-to-day driving: dispatch actions, <strong class="text-zinc-200">decide
                approvals</strong>, mint their own MCP keys, and write runbooks — create, edit,
                publish, discard — as long as every step stays inside their own runner and pack
                access. Plus viewing runners, runs, policies, and audit. No team, policy, or trust
                management, and deleting a runbook stays with admins.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Viewer</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Read-only: see runners, runs, approvals, runbooks, policies, and audit. Can't
                dispatch or decide anything.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="inviting">Inviting your team</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/team/invite"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team → Invite</a>: enter an email, pick the role, send. The invite link is a
        one-time token (hashed at rest like every credential here); accepting it creates the
        membership at the role you chose. Granting the
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">owner</strong>
        role requires an owner — admins invite up
        to their own level. Members can be suspended (sessions killed immediately, sign-in
        blocked) and unsuspended without losing their history.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-scopes">Per-member scope: runners and packs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Roles answer <em>what</em>
        someone can do; runner scopes answer <em>where</em>. The owner who
        created the account sees every runner; everyone you invite — and everyone provisioned through
        SSO — starts with <strong class="text-zinc-200">no runner access</strong>
        and is granted up. Open the member from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team</a>
        and add scopes; they see the union of what you list:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Group scope</strong>
            — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">group: cassandra-prod</code>.
            Follows the group: new runners joining it are automatically in scope.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runner scope</strong> — one specific host, for the
            contractor who should touch exactly one box.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then narrow what they can run there. A grant is
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">all packs</strong>
        by default, or an explicit list — so the DBA who legitimately manages the database fleet
        can run <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">postgres.*</code>
        on those hosts without also getting <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">shell</code>. Runner groups can't
        express that on their own: the packs you most want to withhold are the ones installed
        everywhere. The two dimensions intersect — the member reaches a runner only if it is in
        their runner scope, and can run an action there only if its pack is in their pack scope.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Scoping is treated as <strong class="text-zinc-200">existence-hiding</strong>, not just
        permission-denying: out-of-scope runners don't appear in lists, their detail pages 404,
        and their actions vanish from the member's dispatch surface. Out-of-scope packs drop out
        of the same discovery and dispatch surfaces, while run history for a runner they do reach
        stays whole — an operator never reads a partial record of their own hosts. An API key
        inherits its owner's scope, so "the DBA's agent only reaches <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">cassandra-*</code>" falls out
        of scoping the DBA — not a policy convention, and not a per-key setting to keep in sync.
        Scope changes are audited.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Scope binds owners and admins too. An admin scoped to one group really does lose the rest
        of the fleet. Nobody can change their own access, but anyone who can manage them can
        change it for them.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="mfa">MFA</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        TOTP (any standard authenticator app) with one-time recovery codes — codes are shown
        once, stored hashed, and each works exactly once. Replayed TOTP codes are rejected.
        Beyond self-service, an owner or admin can flip
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require MFA</strong>
        on the account: members without MFA
        are routed to enrollment before they can touch anything else. On Team or Enterprise, a
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >
          single sign-on
        </.link>
        session can satisfy that requirement when your IdP enforces MFA.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sessions">Sessions &amp; credentials</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Profile shows every active session with device info; sign out any of them — or
            everywhere except the current one — in a click. Suspension and admin-forced sign-out
            disconnect live dashboards immediately, not at next page load.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Magic-link sign-in uses single-use, expiring, hashed email tokens — a browser-held
            nonce plus an emailed 6-character code, so an intercepted email can't sign in on its own.
            Sign-in is rate-limited per IP and per email, and failed attempts land in the audit
            log on your account, so probing is visible to admins.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            An API key is identity + attribution — what it may run is your account <strong>policy</strong>. Keys are revocable from <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >LLM agents</a>; raw secrets
            are shown once, only hashes are stored.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Keys self-rotate near expiry — the client installs a successor and swaps to it with no
            interruption — and revoking or suspending the member who minted a key revokes it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
